<h2 class="wp-block-heading">How to Decorate Above Kitchen Cabinets in 6 Steps</h2>



<ol class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Clear and clean the top.</strong> Take everything down and wipe off the dust and grease. A sheet of shelf liner up top (away from the range) makes the next cleaning easier.</li>



<li><strong>Measure the gap.</strong> Note the height to the ceiling and the depth of the cabinet top so you buy pieces that fit.</li>



<li><strong>Mark the no-go zone.</strong> Leave the space next to the range hood and above the cooktop empty.</li>



<li><strong>Pick one material family.</strong> For example woven baskets plus stoneware, or greenery plus wood, so the line of decor looks calm.</li>



<li><strong>Arrange in groups.</strong> Set pieces in two or three groups of odd numbers, with empty space between them.</li>



<li><strong>Check from the floor.</strong> Step back to the doorway. If it looks busy, remove the smallest pieces first.</li>
</ol>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Decor Ideas for Above Kitchen Cabinets</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">1. Large Woven Baskets</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lidded seagrass or rattan baskets are one of the easiest choices. They add warmth and texture, read clearly from below and can quietly hold light, rarely used things like spare napkins or party supplies. Line up three in different heights for a relaxed look.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-02-lidded-baskets.jpg" alt="Three lidded seagrass baskets in different sizes lined up on top of white kitchen cabinets below a white ceiling, with a trailing plant at one end" class="wp-image-1530" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-02-lidded-baskets.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-02-lidded-baskets-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-02-lidded-baskets-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-02-lidded-baskets-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">2. Oversized Stoneware Crocks and Jugs</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Big stoneware crocks, jugs and terracotta urns suit farmhouse and cottage kitchens. Their size is the point: from the floor, one large crock looks intentional, while a row of small jars looks like clutter. Add a few olive branches or dried hydrangeas to one of them for height.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-03-stoneware-crocks.jpg" alt="Oversized stoneware crocks with olive branches and dried hydrangeas, a terracotta urn and a wooden dough bowl standing upright on top of cream kitchen cabinets under a wood beam" class="wp-image-1531" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-03-stoneware-crocks.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-03-stoneware-crocks-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-03-stoneware-crocks-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-03-stoneware-crocks-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">3. Wooden Dough Bowls and Boards</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A vintage dough bowl or a large round wooden board stood upright against the wall adds a warm, curved shape behind crocks or baskets. Lean it at the back so it is less likely to slide forward.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">4. Faux Trailing Greenery</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Trailing greenery spilling over the edge softens the hard line of the cabinets. Faux plants are the practical choice up high, since real plants are hard to water there and many need more light. If you do use real plants and have pets, check them first: the ASPCA lists <a href="https://www.aspca.org/pet-care/animal-poison-control/toxic-and-non-toxic-plants/golden-pothos" target="_blank" rel="noopener">golden pothos as toxic to dogs and cats</a>.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-04-trailing-greenery.jpg" alt="Sage green upper cabinets topped with several pots of faux trailing greenery spilling over the edge, above a white subway tile backsplash, a farmhouse sink and a wooden island with a vase of eucalyptus" class="wp-image-1532" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-04-trailing-greenery.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-04-trailing-greenery-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-04-trailing-greenery-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-04-trailing-greenery-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">5. Leaning Art and Botanical Prints</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">In a tall gap, two framed prints leaned against the wall make the space feel like a gallery ledge. Botanical prints in soft greens and creams suit most kitchens. Pair them with a board or a plant so they are not the only thing up there.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-06-prints-board.jpg" alt="Two framed botanical prints and a large round wooden board leaning on the wall above cream kitchen cabinets, with faux trailing greenery and a stoneware jug of greenery" class="wp-image-1534" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-06-prints-board.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-06-prints-board-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-06-prints-board-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-06-prints-board-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">6. Platters and Plates on Stands</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A few large platters or decorative plates on plate stands add color and pattern. Choose pieces you rarely use, since getting them down means climbing up.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Light and Minimal Options</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">7. Hidden LED Strip Lights</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A warm LED strip hidden on top of the cabinets bounces light off the ceiling and makes the whole kitchen glow in the evening. It works in any gap, even a small one. LEDs are also efficient: the U.S. Department of Energy notes that <a href="https://www.energy.gov/energysaver/led-lighting" target="_blank" rel="noopener">residential LEDs</a>, especially ENERGY STAR-rated ones, use at least 75% less energy and last up to 25 times longer than incandescent lighting. Use a plug-in kit near an outlet or ask an electrician for a hardwired option.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-05-led-glow.jpg" alt="Oak kitchen at dusk with a warm LED strip hidden on top of the upper cabinets lighting the ceiling, a few stoneware vases and a trailing plant above the cabinets, under-cabinet lights over a stone backsplash and an island with rush stools" class="wp-image-1533" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-05-led-glow.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-05-led-glow-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-05-led-glow-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-05-led-glow-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">8. Leave It Empty on Purpose</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Not every gap needs filling. If the gap is small or the kitchen already has a lot going on, a clean empty line, a painted wall or crown molding can look more modern than any decor. This is also the lowest-maintenance choice.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Practical Storage Up Top</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">9. Matching Bins for Rarely Used Items</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The top of the cabinets can hold things you use once or twice a year, like light holiday linens or a spare cake carrier, as long as they are hidden in matching lidded bins or baskets. Keep everyday items low: the CDC’s <a href="https://www.cdc.gov/steadi/pdf/steadi_checkforsafety_brochure-print.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">home fall-prevention checklist</a> advises keeping things you use often on lower shelves, about waist high. For more ways to free up cabinet space, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-organize-kitchen-cabinets/">kitchen cabinet organization guide</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/kitchen-storage-ideas/">kitchen storage ideas</a>.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">10. Store Holiday Pieces Out of Sight</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lidded baskets above the cabinets are a handy spot for seasonal serving pieces. Keep heavy items out of them, and label the lids. Our guide to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/christmas-decoration-storage/">Christmas decoration storage</a> covers the rest of the holiday gear.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Styling Rules That Make It Work</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">11. Group in Odd Numbers With Gaps</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Arrange decor in two or three groups of one to three pieces, and leave empty space between groups. A continuous row of objects from wall to wall looks like storage, while spaced groups look styled.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">12. Keep the Range Area Clear</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Steam, grease and heat from cooking collect above the range, and baskets, dried stems and paper are things that can catch fire. Leave the cabinet tops on either side of the hood empty, and keep everything well away from the cooktop.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">13. Repeat Colors From the Kitchen</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Pick colors that already appear below: the wood tone of your counters or floor, the cream of your backsplash or the green of a plant. Repeating them up high ties the whole wall together. If you are styling the counters too, our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/kitchen-counter-decor-ideas/">kitchen counter decor ideas</a> use the same approach.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">14. Swap One Group for the Season</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For fall, swap one group for white and sage pumpkins, a jug of dried wheat and a basket. In winter, use faux greenery and a few pinecones. Keep seasonal pieces away from the range, and keep the change small so it stays easy to put away.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-07-fall.jpg" alt="Fall decor above sage green kitchen cabinets: white and green pumpkins, a stoneware jug of dried wheat, a woven basket and trailing greenery, with a white tile backsplash and styled counter below" class="wp-image-1535" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-07-fall.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-07-fall-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-07-fall-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-07-fall-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Mistakes to Avoid Above Kitchen Cabinets</h2>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Too many small items.</strong> From the floor they look like clutter and they all collect dust.</li>



<li><strong>Decor above the range.</strong> Heat, steam and grease reach that area, and the U.S. Fire Administration advises keeping anything that can catch fire away from the stovetop.</li>



<li><strong>Using a chair to reach up.</strong> The CDC checklist says to use a step stool with a bar to hold on to and never a chair.</li>



<li><strong>Heavy things near the edge.</strong> Push pieces toward the wall so they are harder to knock off.</li>



<li><strong>Forgetting about dust.</strong> Plan a quick wipe every month or two, and choose pieces that are easy to lift down.</li>
</ul>



<figure class="wp-block-image size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" width="1024" height="1024" src="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-08-before-after.jpg" alt="Above kitchen cabinets before and after: the top of white cabinets crowded with boxes, bottles, a toolbox and plastic tubs, then the same space with two woven baskets, a stoneware jug and a trailing plant" class="wp-image-1536" srcset="https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-08-before-after.jpg 1024w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-08-before-after-300x300.jpg 300w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-08-before-after-150x150.jpg 150w, https://dailyspacehacks.com/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/above-kitchen-cabinet-decor-08-before-after-768x768.jpg 768w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px" /></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If the space up there has turned into overflow storage, start with our guide on <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/how-to-declutter-your-home/">how to declutter your home</a>, then decide what really needs to stay in the kitchen.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Is decorating above kitchen cabinets outdated?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Crowded rows of small knick-knacks can look dated, but a few large, simple pieces like baskets, stoneware or greenery still look fresh. Leaving the space empty is also a valid modern choice.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What can I put on top of my kitchen cabinets?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Large woven baskets, stoneware crocks and jugs, faux trailing greenery, leaning art, wooden boards or hidden LED strip lights. Lidded baskets can also store rarely used items.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How do I keep the top of my cabinets from getting dusty?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Lay shelf liner on top, away from the range, so you can swap it out, choose fewer pieces that are easy to lift down and give the area a quick wipe every month or two.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What if there is only a small gap above my cabinets?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use low pieces like small jugs or flat greenery, or skip decor and add a warm LED strip. In a very small gap, leaving it empty often looks best.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For the wall space below the cabinets, see our <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/kitchen-wall-decor-ideas/">kitchen wall decor ideas</a> so the whole wall feels like one plan.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading">Final Thoughts</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The best over kitchen cabinet decorating ideas are simple: measure the gap, choose a few large pieces in one material family, group them with space between and keep the range area clear. Start with what you already own, like a basket or a big jug, and add more only if the space still looks empty.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">For more kitchen ideas, read our guides to <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/kitchen-counter-organization/">kitchen counter organization</a>, <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/diy-floating-shelves/">DIY floating shelves</a> and <a href="https://dailyspacehacks.com/pantry-organization-ideas/">pantry organization ideas</a>.</p>



